# CANONICAL
d.I("(b) a household in which a runaway's master and a fellow-worker named Archippus will reappear in Philemon.","(b) a household in which Onesimus, there \"no longer as a slave\" (Phlm 16), and a fellow-worker named Archippus will reappear in Philemon. That Onesimus was a slave is Philemon's information, not Colossians' `[S: audit 5]`; \"runaway\" is a reconstruction `[S]`.")
d.R('- Ephesians depends on Colossians (Lincoln; Witherington);',"- Ephesians depends on Colossians (Lincoln; Witherington; and, from an owned commentary, Dunn: \"written using Colossians as a kind of template (so most)\", with Mitton and against Coutts `[S: Logos 5]`);")
# PRESENTING SITUATION: authorship
d.I("Whether Paul wrote the letter, or a later disciple in his name, is disputed in modern scholarship `[S]`. Positions recorded:","Whether Paul wrote the letter, or a later disciple in his name, is disputed in modern scholarship `[S]`. **v1.2 — Patrick's own commentators.** What each *adopts*, from the pasted conclusions `[S: Logos 5]`:\n\n| Commentator | Author | Place | Date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wright (TNTC) | Paul | Ephesus | early 50s |\n| Pao (ZECNT) | Paul, \"the best hypothesis\" | Rome | AD 60–62, with Philemon |\n| Osborne | Paul | Rome, \"definitely the most likely\" | AD 60–62 |\n| Witherington | Paul's voice; Timothy probably the secretary; the style chosen for the Lycus valley | not discussed | not given |\n| Bird (NCCS) | Paul, \"written in association with others … especially Timothy\" | Ephesus, \"marginally less problematic\" — stated for Philemon; that Colossians goes with it is `[I]` | c. 55–57 — stated for Philemon |\n| Dunn (NIGTC) | Probably composed by Timothy as Paul's secretary, \"with a fair degree of license\", with Paul adding 4:18. If Paul approved it, it is \"'Pauline' in the full sense of the word\" | \"inclined toward\" Rome (his p. 40 not yet seen) | about the same time as Philemon |\n\n**On authorship they divide only on how much of the composition is Paul's own**, and all six put the letter in Paul's lifetime. **On place they split, between Rome and Ephesus, and the date follows the place.** Dunn's n. 47 names the post-Pauline side as the majority (Lohse, Gnilka, Pokorný, Wolter and others), so these six are not a sample of the field.\n\nOther positions recorded, from introductions and not from the owned commentaries:")
d.DEL('- **Dunn:** Timothy writing during Paul')
d.R('The first four come through introductions',"These four are `[S: Logos 3]`. The letter's own claim is plain, and it is the canonical reader's starting point `[T]`.")
d.I("The place (Rome, Ephesus or Caesarea) is not stated `[S]`.","The place is not stated `[T]`; the owned commentators hold Rome or Ephesus (above), and Caesarea is reported `[S]`. Whether \"fellow prisoner\" (4:10) is literal is open: Dunn and Wright lean literal, Pao figurative, and none uses the title to fix the place `[S: Logos 5]`.")
# LUKAN
d.R("**The letter's idiom, as the audits found it.**","**The letter's idiom, as the audits found it.** Of the external links proposed across five audits, what survives is mostly Paul's own vocabulary, or idiom he shares with the early church. The sharpest case is **the Lukan conversion idiom of 1:12–14**, *moderate–high* `[S: audit 5]`:")
d.R('- Acts 26:18 at 1:12–14 — six of the rarer',"- Acts 26:18 — six of the rarer lemmas of 1:12–14 in one verse, against three for any other NT verse. Of all Pauline three-verse passages, only one other matches a single verse of Luke–Acts so closely: 1 Cor 11:22–25 with Luke 22:19–20, the Last Supper words;\n- Luke 22:53 — ἡ ἐξουσία τοῦ σκότους (\"the authority of darkness\"), elsewhere in the NT only at Col 1:13;\n- Acts 8:21 — μερίς (\"portion\") with κλῆρος (\"lot\"), elsewhere in the NT only at Col 1:12;")
d.R('- Luke 1:68–79 at 1:13–14;',"- Luke 1:68–79 at 1:13–14 — *moderate*, as idiom. ῥύομαι (\"rescue\") with a λυτρ- word (\"redeem\") is within chance on its own; the cluster is rare only with forgiveness and darkness (all SBLGNT).")
d.R('- Luke 8:15 at 1:6–11 (SBLGNT)',"\n**Across the letter as a whole, Colossians is the least Lukan of the thirteen Pauline letters** `[S: audit 5]`. So \"the Lukan affinities of Colossians\" is *low*, and Luke 8:15 moves to the shared sower tradition (row 15).")
d.R('Luke is with Paul at the close (4:14)',"Luke is with Paul at the close (4:14) `[T]`. Dunn reports Aletti, who \"wonders whether it points to Lukan authorship\", and Pao reads Acts 26:18 as confessional wording `[S: Logos 5]`. Acts 26:18 stands on Christ's lips as Paul's commission, so the contact may be Luke reporting Paul's own conversion vocabulary. Any inference about authorship stays `[I]`.")
# ARC
d.R('**The personal frame (proposal).**',"**The personal frame (proposal) — v1.2, restated.** 4:2–13 picks up the vocabulary of Paul's labour in 1:25–2:7 `[T]` (verified by lemma):\n- μυστήριον (\"mystery\") with φανερόω (\"make manifest\") within two verses, at 1:26–27 and 4:3–4, and elsewhere in the NT only at 1 Tim 3:16 — the frame's sharpest strand;\n- ἀγωνίζομαι (\"strive\") and τέλειος (\"mature\"), each only in 1:28–29 and 4:12 in the letter;\n- πληροφορέω (\"fully assure\", 4:12) as the cognate of πληροφορία (\"full assurance\", 2:2).\n\nEpaphras's prayer also echoes the opening prayer: θέλημα (\"will\") comes from 1:9, not from this unit. The greetings of 4:14–18 add nothing.\n\nOf twelve Pauline letter-closings, this is the strongest link to an earlier stretch. Within the letter, 1.7 % of window pairs share as many rarer lemmas (at most 50 NT verses), 5.5 % at 100 and 22.8 % at 20, so it does not hold at the strictest threshold. The place-name Laodicea helps: 5.1 % without it at the 50-verse threshold. The comparison with Romans is thematic only. Pao names the links (pp. 315–16) `[S: Logos 5]`. *Moderate* `[S: audit 5]`.")
d.R('**3:12–17 as the summary of church life (proposal).**',"**3:12–17 — v1.2, restated.** The earlier claim, that this unit draws on more of the letter than any other, fails: it is never first on any measure, and 1:24–2:5 and 2:6–15 reach further (*low*) `[S: audit 5]`. Reframed, at *moderate*: 3:12–17 gathers words from the opening prayer, Paul's ministry, the forgiveness of 2:13, the polemic and the putting-off, and turns the polemic's ταπεινοφροσύνη (\"humility\") to good. It does **not** reach the confession of 1:15–20. It is the last lexically integrated unit before the household code, which shares no rarer lemma with any other unit `[S: audit 5]`.\n\nAudit 5 lists σύνδεσμος (\"bond\", 2:19 → 3:14) as re-valued too. This overview does not follow it there: the word is not pejorative at 2:19, and audit 2 calls the link a continuation, not a reversal (Echo 44). The point stays open under C78.\n\n**The joints between the units — v1.2.** The twelve units' joints carry *less* rare vocabulary than an average cut point, as one would expect where paragraphs break. The letter's strong verbal links run at a distance (Cross-Pericope Macro-Arguments, 5) `[S: audit 5]`.")
d.save()
